Yes, here are the steps to implement token-based authentication:

1. Generate a token: When a user successfully logs into the system, generate a token that contains the user's credentials and role (if they have any). 2. Include the token in requests: All requests from the user to the server should include the token in an Authorization header. This token should be sent through HTTPS to prevent interception. 3. Verify token on the server: On the server, verify the token for validity and check the user's role (if they have any). This can be done using a JWT (JSON Web Token) library such as `jwt.io` or Angular's `@auth0/angular-jwt`

4. Authorize user: If the token is valid and the user's role permits the action, authorize the user to perform the desired action. Here is an example of how to carry out token-based authentication using Angular's `@auth0/angular-jwt`:

1. Install the dependency by running the following command:
```
npm install @auth0/angular-jwt
```

2. Create a token service that will generate and store the token:
```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { JwtHelperService } from '@auth0/angular-jwt';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root',
})
export class TokenService {
  private TOKEN_KEY = 'auth-token';

  constructor(private jwtHelper: JwtHelperService) {}

  setToken(token: string) {
    localStorage.setItem(this.TOKEN_KEY, token);
  }

  getToken(): string {
    return localStorage.getItem(this.TOKEN_KEY);
  }

  removeToken() {
    localStorage.removeItem(this.TOKEN_KEY);
  }

  isAuthenticated(): boolean {
    const token = this.getToken();
    return !this.jwtHelper.isTokenExpired(token);
  }
}
```

3. Create an auth interceptor that will include the token in requests:
```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpInterceptor, HttpHandler, HttpRequest } from '@angular/common/http';
import { TokenService } from './token.service';

@Injectable()
export class AuthInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  constructor(private tokenService: TokenService) {}

  intercept(req: HttpRequest<any>, next: HttpHandler) {
    const authReq = req.clone({
      headers: req.headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${this.tokenService.getToken()}`),
    });

    return next.handle(authReq);
  }
}
```

4. Register the auth interceptor in your app.module.ts file:
```typescript
import { AuthInterceptor } from './auth.interceptor';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [...],
  imports: [...],
  providers: [
    ...
    { provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS, useClass: AuthInterceptor, multi: true },
  ],
  bootstrap: [...],
})
export class AppModule {}
```

By following these steps, you can implement token-based authentication in your Angular app to ensure that only authorized users can perform sensitive actions.

