The parallax effect is created by manipulating the perspective and scale of the child div in the .parallax div. The child div is moved back along the z-axis and scaled up, creating the appearance of depth and motion as you scroll down the page.

Note that for a full parallax effect, you may need to adjust SVG image height and make sure it is suitable for your site’s design. You also may want to add additional images, text, JQuery or JS scripts to prettify it or have more complex movement.

Below is an example of a multi-threaded C++ program that prints the odd numbers from 0 to 100. The C++ standard library `std::mutex` is used for synchronization.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx;  // Mutex for critical section

// Function to print odd numbers
void printOdd(int start, int end) {
    for(int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
        if(i % 2 == 1) {
            mtx.lock();   // Locking the mutex
            std::cout << "Thread " << std::this_thread::get_id() << ": "<< i << std::endl;
            mtx.unlock();  // Unlocking the mutex
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(printOdd, 0, 50);
    std::thread t2(printOdd, 51, 100);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    return 0;
}
```

In the above program, we have two threads `t1` is responsible for printing odd numbers in the range 0 to 50 and `t2` is responsible for printing odd numbers in the range 51 to 100. The `std::mutex` `mtx` is used to ensure that only one thread can access the critical section (print to the console) at a time. Simply the lock is acquired before entering the critical section and released after the critical section.

Note: The order in which the numbers are printed is not guaranteed because the OS scheduler decides which thread runs when. This means numbers may not necessarily print in numerical order.

