Below is a C++ program with the requested 'remove_vowels' function. Please note that this program treats 'y' as a consonant because 'y' is only sometimes considered a vowel.

```cpp
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

std::string remove_vowels(std::string str) {
    std::string vowels = "aeiouAEIOU";
    for (int i = 0; i < vowels.length(); i++) {
        str.erase(std::remove(str.begin(), str.end(), vowels[i]), str.end());
    }
    return str;
}

int main() {
    // Testing an empty string
    printf("Output of removing vowels from empty string: %s\n", remove_vowels("").c_str());

    // Testing a combination of alphabets with "\n" as a delimiter
    printf("Output of removing vowels from 'a\nb\nc\nd\ne': %s\n", remove_vowels("a\nb\nc\nd\ne").c_str());

    // Testing a vowel packed string
    printf("Output of removing vowels from 'aeiou': %s\n", remove_vowels("aeiou").c_str());

    // Testing a string with a solo non-vowel character surrounded by vowels
    printf("Output of removing vowels from 'iahi': %s\n", remove_vowels("iahi").c_str());

    // Testing a string devoid of vowels
    printf("Output of removing vowels from 'bcd': %s\n", remove_vowels("bcd").c_str());

    return 0;
}
```
In the 'remove_vowels' function, the algorithm iterates over all the vowels (both lower case and upper case) and for each vowel, it removes all instances of that vowel from the input string. The resulting string, which no longer contains any vowels, is then returned. Note that the empty string result for a vowel-only input string or an actual empty string is expected and correct.
